More on this. This is an issue around environment variables, R, and 
Java, it seems.

An other way to solve the problem is to run the following right before 
starting Python / ipython:

$ R CMD javareconf -e

What seems to be happening is that when one writes "R" in terminal a 
shell script is executed, setting few environment variables before 
calling a binary for R.
LD_LIBRARY_PATH is tweaked at that moment. See:

||cat `R RHOME`/etc/ldpaths

This script is obviously not executed when running an embedded R, which 
explains the difference in behaviour between importing rJava from R, and 
from rpy2 (embedded R).

I have opened an issue ( 
https://bitbucket.org/lgautier/rpy2/issue/158/error-when-importing-rjava ).


L.


